# SYNOPSIS

```c
const char *vx_main(void);
[[noreturn]] void vx_exits(const char *msg);
[[noreturn]] void vx_exit(int n);
[[noreturn]] void vx_abort(void);
vx_strs vx_args(void);
vx_str vx_arg(size_t i);
int vx_argc(void);
char **vx_argv(void);
void vx_random_bytes(void *out, size_t n);
extern uintptr_t __stack_chk_guard;
```

# DESCRIPTION

These are vx-rt's start file, `lib/vx-rt/crt1.c` (M6 step 6e2a, os-requirements R1–R5), which `rt.c` includes; the native target's toolchain links the same code as its `crt1.o`.

The kernel enters `_start` with the task's bootstrap channel.
vx-rt reads the spawn message from it, seeds the process's random generator from its `entropy=` record (with its task id and the clock mixed in), draws `__stack_chk_guard` from it, sets up the main thread's TLS and the standard files, then runs `.preinit_array` and `.init_array` in order and calls `vx_main`.
Its return value is the exit string: `nullptr` or `""` for success, else why it failed, as Plan 9's `exits` takes one.

`vx_exits` ends the program the same way from anywhere: `.fini_array` runs, last to first, then what the program printed is flushed, its pipes are closed and every thread ends.
`vx_exit` is C's `exit`: the empty exit string for 0, else <n> in decimal, as the POSIX personality ends, so rc(1) shows `exit 7` as `name pid: 7`.

`vx_abort` is C's `abort`: standard error is flushed, then the program traps, which ends it as a fault does, with no destructors; procfs(4) saves a crash directory under `/tmp/crash`.
A note the program does not handle also ends it without destructors.

`vx_args` is the arguments as slices, made at start-up (09 §5.1, M6 step 6e4c3): the first is the `argv0=` record, else the program's name, then its `arg=` records. `vx_arg` is one of them, or the zero slice past the last. These are what a native program uses; `vx_argv` is for C's `main`.

`vx_argv` is a C `argv`, made once from the spawn message: `argv[0]` is the `argv0=` record, else the program's name, then its `arg=` records, then a null; `vx_argc` counts them.
The strings share a 16 KiB buffer; arguments past it are left out, never cut in the middle.

`vx_random_bytes` reads from the process's generator.
vx-rt's spawn gives each child a seed of its own from it, as an `entropy=` record, unless the spawner's records carry one.

